From 23a5191e049a4bd14ed1ccfbe763e528f1f8a40b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romain Dolbeau <romain@dolbeau.org>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 09:55:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] m68k pmap (MULTIPROCESSOR): targeted and batched TLB
 shootdowns

- Statistics: per-CPU pmap event counters (vmstat -e, "pmap cpuN"),
  attributed to the pmap entry point in progress.
- lockstat(4) for virt68k MP kernels: cpu_counter() on the virt-smp
  nanosecond counter (__HAVE_CPU_COUNTER with MULTIPROCESSOR).
- No shootdown when a mapping is entered or only gains rights: the
  other CPUs can only have a stale invalid or read-only ATC entry; the
  fault it causes is fixed by a local flush (pmap_fault_fixup() in
  trap() for kernel addresses, pmap_enter() through uvm_fault() for user
  ones).
- Per-pmap mask of the CPUs that have it loaded (pm_cpus); a CPU keeps
  its last user pmap loaded while it runs kernel lwps (ci_pmap), so
  going back to the same process needs no ATC flush.  Shootdowns of a
  user pmap go only to those CPUs; a destroyed pmap is dropped from them.
- pmap_remove() & co batch their remote invalidations (up to 16 VAs,
  then a full flush), one shootdown per call.
- Per-sender shootdown requests instead of one global mailbox, so that
  CPUs can shoot each other down at the same time.

The pmap entry points still take the kernel lock.
